{"claim_id": "R1-74", "task_id": "R11-S14", "claim": "z=infinity is an ORDINARY point of object B's trace-free normal form: under x=1/z the potential is analytic at x=0 with value 11/4 and series 11/4 - 11x/2 - 29x^2/4 + O(x^3). B therefore has three singular points, not four, and Katz multiset (0,0,1).", "label": "PROVEN", "evidence_type": "computation", "dps": null, "reproducible": true, "script": "verify_s14.py", "output_hash": "717f8dc9c6307ff8ec44227117a4170ccc67cac1ee253d06969617aaf59ca7f9", "review": "cold", "review_note": "CT-S14-COLDREAD-B reached this independently under isolation."}
{"claim_id": "R1-75", "task_id": "R11-S14", "claim": "The rank-1 twist h=A^(-1/2) relating the shared normal form to companion v1.2's PRINTED scalar operator alters the trace by 2*dlog(h) = -dlog(A) = -(6x+1)/(3x^2+x+1) != 0, and so falls outside the trace-0 class that van der Put-Saito p. 2634 sets up for Table 2.1.", "label": "PROVEN", "evidence_type": "computation", "dps": null, "reproducible": true, "script": "verify_s14.py", "output_hash": "717f8dc9c6307ff8ec44227117a4170ccc67cac1ee253d06969617aaf59ca7f9", "review": "cold", "review_note": "CT-S14-COLDREAD-B task 4, the question the packet was built around."}
{"claim_id": "R1-76", "task_id": "R11-S14", "claim": "Object B's Table 2.1 row is UNAFFECTED by R1-75: the row is read from the trace-free normal form, which inversion alone reaches (R1-83). The twist is a feature of the companion's printed presentation, not of the classification. The drafted phrase 'up to inversion and twist' was therefore unnecessary and inaccurate, and was removed.", "label": "STRUCTURAL", "evidence_type": "proof", "dps": null, "reproducible": true, "script": "verify_s14.py", "output_hash": "717f8dc9c6307ff8ec44227117a4170ccc67cac1ee253d06969617aaf59ca7f9", "review": "cold", "review_note": "CT-S14 task 5 states the re-normalisation; adopted with the qualification recorded in VERIFY-S14.md that its task-4 phrasing overstated the risk to the row."}
{"claim_id": "R1-77", "task_id": "R11-S14", "claim": "At each root of A=3x^2+x+1 the companion's PRINTED scalar operator has residue(P)=1 and lim (x-b)^2 Q = 0, hence indicial polynomial lambda^2 and exponents [0,0]; in the trace-free normal form the exponents are [1/2,1/2]. Katz invariant 0 in both presentations, so no row is affected.", "label": "PROVEN", "evidence_type": "computation", "dps": null, "reproducible": true, "script": "verify_s14.py", "output_hash": "717f8dc9c6307ff8ec44227117a4170ccc67cac1ee253d06969617aaf59ca7f9", "review": "cold", "review_note": "CT-S14 task 3, arithmetic re-verified here."}
{"claim_id": "R1-78", "task_id": "R11-S14", "claim": "CORRECTION to CT-S14 task 3: it called those points 'apparent/regular singular'. By the C1 lemma, residue(P)=1 with Q at most a simple pole forces a log term, so the points are regular singular and NON-apparent. CT-S14 was isolated from C1 by design and could not have known; the defect is in adoption, not in the reviewer. Substance ('not ordinary') adopted.", "label": "PROVEN", "evidence_type": "proof", "dps": null, "reproducible": true, "script": "verify_s14.py", "output_hash": "717f8dc9c6307ff8ec44227117a4170ccc67cac1ee253d06969617aaf59ca7f9", "review": "self", "review_note": "follows from C1 plus the verified hypotheses in R1-77; not independently reviewed, but it only tightens a label."}
